<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
    What Android's own auto-backup is allowed to take.

    This was an empty template for a long time, which meant the platform silently shipped the whole
    library, the search index and every preference to the user's Google Drive with no UI and no
    consent beyond the OS toggle. Three things must never go:

      * the sync secrets are sealed by a key that lives in *this device's* keystore, so a restored
        copy can never be decrypted — it produces an account that looks configured and fails
        every refresh;
      * the search index is a derived cache that is cheaper to rebuild than to restore stale;
      * the sync state describes files on the device that wrote it. Another device's bookkeeping
        arriving as your own is the one input that makes the planner delete things.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="sync-secrets.xml" />
    <exclude domain="database" path="note-index.db" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="sync/" />
</full-backup-content>
